> > b_j ~ Normal(0, sigma_experiment_interaction)
> >
> > Our Stan model then has a hyperprior that jointly estimates the
> > scales:
> > sigma_1, sigma_2, sigma_3, sigma_experiment ~ Normal^+(0, sigma_main)
> > sigma_1_interaction, sigma_2_interaction, sigma_3_interaction,
> > sigma_experiment_interaction ~ Normal^+(0, sigma_interaction)
> >
> > We have weakly-informative priors on the remaining parameters.
> >
> > Is the above model possible to fit in INLA? (Modifications welcome!)
> >
> > Our modeling task is similar to p.239-244 of Bayesian Data Analysis
> > 3rd edition (http://www.stat.columbia.edu/~gelman/book/). Their model
> > does not logit-transform, though we can as you recommend! In my
> > original conception, we were modeling the treatment effects p_jg1 -
> > p_jg0 directly, which in our particular application area are far
> > enough from -1 and 1 for a Normal to have been acceptable.
> >
> > Thanks again!!
> > Shira
> >
> > On Thursday, August 29, 2019 at 1:23:17 AM UTC-4, Helpdesk wrote:
> > > I agree with Haakon.
> > >
> > > I would guess what you mean is that you have a joint model, one
> > > for
> > > treatment and one for control
> > >
> > > so
> > >
> > > y^t ~ treatment
> > > y^c ~ control
> > >
> > > and then you want to impose a model for the difference as well
> > >
> > > y^t ~ treatment - 1/2 * difference
> > > y^c ~ control   + 1/2 * difference
> > >
> > > where the 'difference' is the difference model
> > >
> > > this is on the logit/probit scale.
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > > On Thu, 2019-08-29 at 05:07 +0000, Haakon Bakka wrote:
> > > > Hi,
> > > >
> > > > Are you sure this is what you want? A Gaussian distribution can
> > > be
> > > > larger than 1, but the difference between two probabilities
> > > cannot.
> > > > PS: For us, it is easier to answer the question if you write it
> > > fully
> > > > out as a GAM/GLMM.
> > > >
> > > > Haakon
> > > >
> > > >
> > > >
